dinates 101-700 most naturally refer to this transcript sequence. Given sequence length 102, CDS end 700 exceeds it → internally inconsistent. Even if it were genome coordinates, we can't evaluate without a genome. The task explicitly says to report such inconsistencies rather than repair. Good — flag.\n\nAlso, is the FASTA well-formed? Single-line sequence, header with spaces. Fine. Check for lowercase or ambiguity codes — all uppercase ACGT. Primer CSV: all valid.\n\nNow, let me write the files.\n\nPlan:\n1. Create output/ directory.\n2. Write output/analysis.py.\n3. Run it (Python availability — need to verify: `python --version`).\n4. Verify output/primer_audit.csv and output/report.md.\n5. Present summary.\n\nLet me first check Python availability. Also verify the exact file contents (byte-level) to avoid hidden-character surprises.\n\nLet me write analysis.py carefully.\n\nScript design:\n\n```python\n#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"Primer pair audit against supplied transcript isoforms.\n\nReads:\n  inputs/primer_candidates.csv  (pair_id, forward, reverse, expected_transcript, expected_product_bp)\n  inputs/transcripts.fa         (FASTA with header metadata, e.g. CDS=101-700)\n\nWrites:\n  output/primer_audit.csv\n  output/report.md\n\nDesign rule: supplied sequences and metadata are used as-is. Malformed or\ninternally inconsistent metadata (e.g. a CDS range that exceeds the sequence\nlength) is reported, never silently repaired.\n\"\"\"\n\nimport csv\nimport re\nimport sys\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\nBASE = Path(__file__).resolve().parent.parent   # workspace root\nINPUTS = BASE / \"inputs\"\nOUTPUT = BASE / \"output\"\n\nCOMPLEMENT = str.maketrans(\"ACGT\", \"TGCA\")\n\ndef revcomp(seq): return seq.translate(COMPLEMENT)[::-1]\n\nVALID_DNA = re.compile(r\"^[ACGT]+$\")\n\ndef parse_fasta(path):\n    \"\"\"Return list of records: dict(id, header, seq, meta, issues).\"\"\"\n    records = []\n    current = None\n    with open(path) as fh:\n        for lineno, line in enumerate(fh, 1):\n            line = line.rstrip(\"\\n\")\n            if not line.strip():\n                continue\n            if line.startswith(\">\"):\n                if current: records.append(current)\n                header = line[1:].strip()\n                tid = header.split()[0] if header else \"\"\n                current = {\"id\": tid, \"header\": header, \"seq\": \"\", \"meta\": {}, \"issues\": [], \"line\": lineno}\n                # parse CDS=a-b\n                m = re.search(r\"CDS=(\\d+)-(\\d+)\", header)\n                if m:\n                    current[\"meta\"][\"cds_start\"] = int(m.group(1))\n                    current[\"meta\"][\"cds_end\"] = int(m.group(2))\n                else:\n                    current[\"issues\"].append(\"no CDS annotation in header\")\n            else:\n                if current is None:\n                    # sequence before header: malformed\n                    ...\n                seq = line.strip().upper()\n                bad = set(seq) - set(\"ACGT\")\n                if bad:\n                    current[\"issues\"].append(f\"invalid characters {sorted(bad)} on line {lineno}\")\n                current[\"seq\"] += seq\n    if current: records.append(current)\n    return records\n```\n\nThen per-record validation: sequence non-empty; CDS consistency:\n- cds_start <= cds_end\n- cds_end <= len(seq) → else issue \"CDS end {cds_end} exceeds sequence length {len}\"\n- cds_start >= 1\n- (cds_end - cds_start + 1) % 3 == 0 → else warn (not necessarily malformed but worth noting) — 600 %3==0, 540%3==0 so no issue here; but if it were off we'd note it.\n- Also if CDS is annotated, we might check whether sequence begins with ATG / ends with stop codon — but CDS starts at 101, so position 101-103 should be the start codon. In canonical, position 101 = ? Sequence ends with GAATGA at positions 97-102: position 97=G,98=A,99=A,100=T,101=G,102=A. So CDS 101-102 = \"GA\" — only 2 nt, not even a full codon. Further evidence of inconsistency. We can add a check: CDS length not multiple of 3 after clamping → but we don't clamp (no repair). We can note: CDS start codon at position 101 is not ATG (positions 101-103 don't exist). Let me keep it simpler: report that the CDS range exceeds the sequence length; that's the core inconsistency. Optionally also note that the annotated CDS length (600/540 nt) cannot fit in a 102-nt sequence.\n\nPrimer matching:\n\n```python\ndef find_all(haystack, needle):\n    idxs = []\n    start = 0\n    while True:\n        i = haystack.find(needle, start)\n        if i == -1: break\n        idxs.append(i+1)  # 1-based\n        start = i+1\n    return idxs\n```\n\nFor each pair and each transcript:\n- fwd_positions = find_all(seq, forward)\n- rev_positions = find_all(seq, revcomp(reverse))  # these are start positions of the RC match; match end = pos + len - 1\n- Amplicons: for f in fwd_positions, for r in rev_positions: r_end = r + len(reverse) - 1; if r_end > f: amplicon f..r_end, length r_end - f + 1. (Require forward strictly upstream; typically require f <= r_start? Standard: forward start < reverse match end; also we'd want the reverse primer binding site (r..r_end) to be downstream of forward: r > f. Let me require r > f.)\n\nCollect per-transcript amplicons.\n\ntranscripts_matched = transcripts with ≥1 valid amplicon. For reporting, if there's exactly one amplicon overall (or on expected), report its length.\n\nStatus logic:\n```\nif expected transcript not in records → status \"expected_transcript_missing\"\nelif no amplicon on any transcript → \"no_binding\"\nelse:\n    amp = amplicon on expected transcript if present\n    if none → \"off_target\" (binds only non-expected)\n    elif len(amplicons on expected) > 1 or multiple positions → \"ambiguous\"\n    elif amp.length != expected_product_bp → \"length_mismatch\"\n    elif cds issue → still pass? hmm\n    else \"pass\"\n```\n\nWhere does CDS incompatibility fit into status? If amplicon length matches but is not within CDS → status might be \"cds_incompatible\". In our data, length already mismatches, so status = length_mismatch for p01/p02. Status should reflect the primary failure; reason captures everything.\n\ncds_compatible logic:\n```\nif no amplicon → \"na\"\nelif expected transcript CDS annotation is internally inconsistent → \"inconsistent_metadata\"\nelif amplicon within CDS (and frame ok?) → \"yes\"\nelse \"no\"\n```\n\nFrame: should cds_compatible require the amplicon to preserve the reading frame / be fully within CDS? Let me define \"yes\" as: amplicon fully contained in CDS range AND CDS annotation is valid. (Not requiring frame since the amplicon being within CDS is the compatibility question; though \"compatible\" might mean the amplicon can be translated in frame... let me keep it simple: containment + valid metadata.)\n\nReason strings:\n- p01: \"Forward and reverse primers each match TX_CANONICAL uniquely (positions 1-20 and 83-102); amplicon = 102 bp, expected 108 bp (delta = -6). Header CDS=101-700 is internally inconsistent: CDS end 700 exceeds sequence length 102; CDS compatibility cannot be evaluated.\"\n- p02: similarly for TX_ALT, 99 vs 104.\n- p03: \"Forward primer (20xA) and reverse primer (20xT) match no supplied transcript; no amplicon.\"\n\nReport.md: overview, input summary, metadata issue section, per-pair audit table, verdicts, methods.\n\nAlso note in report: sequences used as-is; no external data (matches \"use only provided sequences\").\n\nNow, CSV columns exactly: pair_id,transcripts_matched,amplicon_length,cds_compatible,status,reason.\n\nValues:\n- p01: TX_CANONICAL, 102, inconsistent_metadata, length_mismatch, \"...\"\n- p02: TX_ALT, 99, inconsistent_metadata, length_mismatch, \"...\"\n- p03: none, na, na, no_binding, \"...\"\n\nHmm, p03 cds_compatible \"na\". OK.\n\nWait — one more thought about transcripts_matched format. If a pair matches multiple transcripts, we'd join with \";\" — here single values. Good.\n\nShould amplicon_length be the one on expected transcript? Yes.\n\nLet me re-verify: any chance p01 reverse primer matches somewhere in TX_ALT or p02's in canonical (giving cross-transcript amplicons)? p01 RC needs \"...GAATGA\" — only canonical tail. p02 RC needs \"...GAATAG\" — only alt tail. Forward matches both. So no cross amplicons. Also any internal binding of reverse primers? p01 RC = GACCTGACTGACCTGAATGA — needs GAATGA, which is unique. Fine.\n\nAlso potential primer-dimer/self-complementarity — out of scope; the audit is against transcripts. Skip (or mention as out of scope).\n\nLet me also reconsider \"expected_product_bp\" parsing: integer. Fine.\n\nNow let me verify Python exists and run. Also verify exact sequence length with the script.\n\nOne more: the task says output/... — relative to workspace cwd. Create C:\\...\\workspace\\output\\.\n\nAlso \"work only inside the current workspace and use only files under ./inputs\" — fine.\n\nNow let me write analysis.py. I'll write it via PowerShell here-string (careful with quoting) — or write it with Python? Better: use `@\"...\"@ | Set-Content` here-string. Python code has many quotes; PowerShell here-strings handle them fine as long as we don't hit `\"@` at line start. Alternatively, write it via python -c. Simplest robust way: use here-string (single-quoted @' '@ to avoid interpolation) and write with Set-Content -Encoding UTF8. The code contains no here-string terminator. Note: single-quoted here-string @'...'@ treats content literally — good.\n\nLet me draft the full analysis.py:\n\n```python\n#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"Audit primer pairs against supplied transcript isoforms.\n\nInputs (only these, nothing else):\n  inputs/primer_candidates.csv : pair_id, forward, reverse, expected_transcript, expected_product_bp\n  inputs/transcripts.fa        : FASTA; header may carry CDS=<start>-<end> annotation\n\nOutputs:\n  output/primer_audit.csv : pair_id, transcripts_matched, amplicon_length, cds_compatible, status, reason\n  output/report.md        : human-readable audit report\n\nPolicy: supplied sequences and metadata are used exactly as supplied. Malformed or\ninternally inconsistent metadata (e.g. a CDS range extending past the end of the\nsequence) is reported in the CSV/MD outputs -- it is never silently repaired.\nMatching is exact (no mismatches/gaps allowed).\n\"\"\"\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nimport csv\nimport re\nimport sys\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\nWORKSPACE = Path(__file__).resolve().parent.parent\nINPUT_DIR = WORKSPACE / \"inputs\"\nOUTPUT_DIR = WORKSPACE / \"output\"\n\nPRIMER_CSV = INPUT_DIR / \"primer_candidates.csv\"\nFASTA = INPUT_DIR / \"transcripts.fa\"\nOUT_CSV = OUTPUT_DIR / \"primer_audit.csv\"\nOUT_MD = OUTPUT_DIR / \"report.md\"\n\nCOMPLEMENT = str.maketrans(\"ACGT\", \"TGCA\")\nVALID_DNA_RE = re.compile(r\"^[ACGT]+$\")\n\n\ndef revcomp(seq: str) -> str:\n    return seq.translate(COMPLEMENT)[::-1]\n\n\n# ---------------- FASTA parsing and metadata validation ----------------\n\ndef parse_fasta(path: Path):\n    records = []\n    current = None\n    seq_lines = []\n    with path.open(encoding=\"utf-8\") as fh:\n        for lineno, raw in enumerate(fh, 1):\n            line = raw.rstrip(\"\\n\\r\")\n            if not line.strip():\n                continue\n            if line.startswith(\">\"):\n                if current is not None:\n                    current[\"seq\"] = \"\".join(seq_lines).upper()\n                    records.append(current)\n                header = line[1:].strip()\n                parts = header.split()\n                current = {\n                    \"id\": parts[0] if parts else f\"UNKNOWN_LINE{lineno}\",\n                    \"header\": header,\n                    \"seq\": \"\",\n                    \"cds\": None,\n                    \"issues\": [],\n                }\n                seq_lines = []\n                m = re.search(r\"CDS=(\\d+)-(\\d+)\", header)\n                if m:\n                    current[\"cds\"] = (int(m.group(1)), int(m.group(2)))\n                else:\n                    current[\"issues\"].append(\"header has no CDS=<start>-<end> annotation\")\n            else:\n                if current is None:\n                    raise ValueError(f\"{path.name}: sequence data before any header at line {lineno}\")\n                seq = line.strip().upper()\n                bad = sorted(set(seq) - set(\"ACGT\"))\n                if bad:\n                    current[\"issues\"].append(\n                        f\"invalid sequence characters {bad} at line {lineno}\")\n                seq_lines.append(seq)\n    if current is not None:\n        current[\"seq\"] = \"\".join(seq_lines).upper()\n        records.append(current)\n    return records\n\n\ndef validate_record(rec):\n    \"\"\"Flag internally inconsistent metadata; never repair it.\"\"\"\n    seq_len = len(rec[\"seq\"])\n    if seq_len == 0:\n        rec[\"issues\"].append(\"sequence is empty\")\n    if rec[\"cds\"] is not None:\n        start, end = rec[\"cds\"]\n        if start > end:\n            rec[\"issues\"].append(f\"CDS start {start} > CDS end {end}\")\n        if start < 1:\n            rec[\"issues\"].append(f\"CDS start {start} < 1\")\n        if end > seq_len:\n            rec[\"issues\"].append(\n                f\"CDS end {end} exceeds sequence length {seq_len} \"\n                f\"(annotated CDS span {end - start + 1} bp cannot fit in a {seq_len} bp transcript)\")\n        elif (end - start + 1) % 3 != 0:\n            rec[\"issues\"].append(\n                f\"CDS span {end - start + 1} bp is not a multiple of 3\")\n    rec[\"consistent\"] = not rec[\"issues\"]\n    return rec\n```\n\nWait — \"header has no CDS annotation\" is not really an \"issue\" (malformed); it's just absence. In our data both have CDS. Let me track cds=None and treat absence as not an inconsistency issue but note it in the report. Actually simpler: keep a separate \"notes\" vs \"issues\". Keep issues only for actual problems; absence of CDS → cds_compatible = \"na\" with reason. In our data both have CDS.\n\nContinuing:\n\n```python\n# ---------------- primer matching ----------------\n\ndef find_all(haystack: str, needle: str):\n    \"\"\"All 1-based start positions of needle in haystack (exact match).\"\"\"\n    positions = []\n    i = haystack.find(needle)\n    while i != -1:\n        positions.append(i + 1)\n        i = haystack.find(needle, i + 1)\n    return positions\n\n\ndef audit_pair(pair, records_by_id, records_order):\n    fwd = pair[\"forward\"].upper()\n    rev = pair[\"reverse\"].upper()\n    expected = pair[\"expected_transcript\"]\n    expected_bp = pair[\"expected_product_bp\"]\n\n    result = {\n        \"pair_id\": pair[\"pair_id\"],\n        \"expected_transcript\": expected,\n        \"expected_product_bp\": expected_bp,\n        \"matches\": {},        # tx id -> list of amplicons (start, end, length)\n        \"fwd_hits\": {},       # tx id -> [positions]\n        \"rev_hits\": {},       # tx id -> [positions] (sense-strand position of RC match)\n        \"issues\": [],\n    }\n\n    for rec in records_order:\n        tid = rec[\"id\"]\n        f_hits = find_all(rec[\"seq\"], fwd) if fwd else []\n        r_hits = find_all(rec[\"seq\"], revcomp(rev)) if rev else []\n        result[\"fwd_hits\"][tid] = f_hits\n        result[\"rev_hits\"][tid] = r_hits\n        amplicons = []\n        for f in f_hits:\n            for r in r_hits:\n                r_end = r + len(rev) - 1\n                if r > f:  # reverse primer binding site downstream of forward primer\n                    amplicons.append((f, r_end, r_end - f + 1))\n        if amplicons:\n            result[\"matches\"][tid] = amplicons\n    return result\n```\n\nHmm wait: condition r > f — should also ensure amplicon isn't absurdly small; r > f is enough for this audit.\n\nThen evaluate:\n\n```python\ndef evaluate(pair, result, records_by_id):\n    tid_exp = result[\"expected_transcript\"]\n    matched = [t for t in records_by_id if result[\"matches\"].get(t)]\n    ...\n```\n\nLet me structure the main():\n\n- Parse fasta, validate\n- Parse primer CSV (validate DNA, integer expected_product_bp)\n- For each pair: audit_pair → evaluate → row\n- Write CSV + report\n\nEvaluate logic:\n\n```python\ndef evaluate(pair, audit, records_by_id):\n    pair_id = pair[\"pair_id\"]\n    expected = pair[\"expected_transcript\"]\n    expected_bp = pair[\"expected_product_bp\"]\n    matched_txs = list(audit[\"matches\"].keys())\n\n    reasons = []\n    status = None\n    amplicon_length = \"\"\n    transcripts_matched = \";\".join(matched_txs) if matched_txs else \"none\"\n\n    rec_exp = records_by_id.get(expected)\n    exp_amps = audit[\"matches\"].get(expected, [])\n\n    if rec_exp is None:\n        status = \"expected_transcript_missing\"\n        reasons.append(f\"expected transcript '{expected}' is not among supplied sequences\")\n    elif not matched_txs:\n        status = \"no_binding\"\n        # detail which primer failed where\n        ...\n    ...\n```\n\nLet me write out the concrete detailed logic with our three cases in mind but keep it generic.\n\nCase p03: no hits anywhere. Reason: \"Forward primer matches 0 of 2 supplied transcripts; reverse-complemented reverse primer matches 0 of 2 supplied transcripts; no amplicon can be formed.\" Status no_binding.\n\nCase p01: exp_amps = [(1,102,102)]. matched_txs=[TX_CANONICAL]. Amplicon length 102 ≠ 108 → status \"length_mismatch\". Also note unique binding positions. CDS: rec_exp has issues → cds_compatible \"inconsistent_metadata\", reason includes the metadata issue. Also note: even taking CDS=101-700 at face value, amplicon 1-102 is mostly outside CDS.\n\nCase p02: similar, 99 vs 104.\n\nAdditional checks: multiple amplicons on expected → ambiguous; matches only on non-expected → off_target. Implement these branches too.\n\nAlso specificity note: p01 forward binds both transcripts but only canonical produces a product — mention in reason/report that the forward is shared and the reverse confers isoform specificity? Nice detail for the report. Compute per-primer cross-reactivity for the report.\n\ncds_compatible determination:\n\n```python\ndef cds_verdict(rec, amps):\n    # amps: amplicons on rec\n    if not amps: return \"na\", \"no amplicon to evaluate\"\n    if rec is None: return \"na\", ...\n    if rec[\"cds\"] is None: return \"na\", \"no CDS annotation supplied\"\n    if rec has inconsistency issues (cds-related): return \"inconsistent_metadata\", ...\n    start, end = rec[\"cds\"]\n    if all(a_start >= start and a_end <= end for amp in amps): return \"yes\"\n    return \"no\"\n```\n\nNote: rec[\"issues\"] might include non-CDS issues (invalid characters). For cds_verdict we should look specifically at CDS-related inconsistency. Let me track cds_issues separately. Let me restructure: rec[\"cds_issues\"] list; rec[\"seq_issues\"] list. consistent = no issues.\n\nFor our records: cds_issues = [\"CDS end 700 exceeds sequence length 102 ...\"].\n\nReport.md content plan:\n\n# Primer audit report\n- Date, tool line\n## 1. Inputs\n- primer_candidates.csv: 3 pairs (table)\n- transcripts.fa: 2 transcripts with length, header, CDS annotation\n## 2. Metadata integrity check (must be prominent)\n- TX_CANONICAL: header claims CDS=101-700 (span 600 bp) but sequence length is 102 bp → internally inconsistent; used as-is, no repair.\n- TX_ALT: CDS=101-640 (span 540 bp) vs length 102 → inconsistent.\n- Consequences: cds_compatible cannot be resolved to yes/no for amplicons on these transcripts; flagged as inconsistent_metadata.\n## 3. Methods\n- Exact matching, reverse primer matched as reverse complement, amplicon = forward start .. reverse match end, length formula; only supplied sequences used; no external references; no repair of metadata.\n## 4. Per-pair results\nTable + per-pair detail paragraphs.\n## 5. Summary verdicts\n- No pair fully passes: