print a console summary.\n\nNote the Windows PowerShell single-quoted here-string: @'\n...content...\n'@ — content must not contain a line starting with \"'@\". Fine.\n\nActually, for a long script, safer to write in chunks or use Out-File with UTF8 without BOM? PowerShell 5.1 Set-Content -Encoding UTF8 writes BOM — Python 3 handles BOM with utf-8-sig? Actually, Python's default utf-8 codec fails on BOM? No — Python 3 does NOT strip BOM when reading source with utf-8... actually Python 3 does accept UTF-8 BOM in source files (tokenizer handles BOM). Yes, CPython permits BOM. But report.md/CSV is written by Python with encoding=\"utf-8\" (no BOM) — fine.\n\nAlternatively use `[System.IO.File]::WriteAllText` to avoid BOM. Let me use WriteAllText without BOM for cleanliness. Or just use Set-Content -Encoding utf8 (BOM) — Python source OK with BOM. Let me use WriteAllText to be safe.\n\nNow let me write the full script body.\n\nAlso: should analysis.py be runnable from anywhere? It uses Path(__file__).parent.parent as workspace root → output and inputs relative to workspace. Since analysis.py is at output/analysis.py, parent.parent = workspace. Good.\n\nLet me now write out the full code:\n\n```python\n#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"...\"\"\"\nfrom __future__ import annotations\nimport csv\nimport re\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\nWORKSPACE = Path(__file__).resolve().parent.parent\nINPUT_DIR = WORKSPACE / \"inputs\"\nOUTPUT_DIR = WORKSPACE / \"output\"\n...\n\nCOMPLEMENT = str.maketrans(\"ACGT\", \"TGCA\")\n\n\ndef revcomp(seq):\n    return seq.translate(COMPLEMENT)[::-1]\n\n\ndef parse_fasta(path):\n    ...as above, with cds_issues/seq_issues...\n\n\ndef validate_record(rec):\n    n = len(rec[\"seq\"])\n    if n == 0:\n        rec[\"seq_issues\"].append(\"sequence is empty\")\n    cds = rec[\"cds\"]\n    if cds is not None:\n        s, e = cds\n        if s < 1:\n            rec[\"cds_issues\"].append(f\"CDS start {s} is below 1\")\n        if s > e:\n            rec[\"cds_issues\"].append(f\"CDS start {s} exceeds CDS end {e}\")\n        elif e > n:\n            rec[\"cds_issues\"].append(\n                f\"annotated CDS={s}-{e} extends past the end of the {n} bp sequence \"\n                f\"(CDS span {e - s + 1} bp cannot fit in {n} bp)\")\n        elif (e - s + 1) % 3 != 0:\n            rec[\"cds_issues\"].append(f\"CDS span {e - s + 1} bp is not a multiple of 3\")\n    rec[\"consistent\"] = not rec[\"cds_issues\"] and not rec[\"seq_issues\"]\n```\n\nHmm — for cds_compatible, we care about cds_issues and seq_issues (if the sequence characters are invalid, matching is compromised too). Our data has neither seq_issues.\n\nfind_all, audit_pair as above.\n\nMain evaluation per pair:\n\n```python\ndef evaluate_pair(pair, audit, records):\n    rec_by_id = {r[\"id\"]: r for r in records}\n    expected = pair[\"expected_transcript\"]\n    expected_bp = pair[\"expected_product_bp\"]\n    matched = list(audit[\"matches\"].keys())\n\n    status_reasons = []\n    # which transcripts exist\n    exp_rec = rec_by_id.get(expected)\n\n    if exp_rec is None:\n        amps_exp = []\n    else:\n        amps_exp = audit[\"matches\"].get(expected, [])\n\n    # choose reported amplicon\n    amp_len = \"\"\n    if matched:\n        if amps_exp:\n            # report the amplicon on the expected transcript\n            if len(amps_exp) == 1 and len(matched) == 1:\n                a = amps_exp[0]\n                amp_len = a[2]\n            else:\n                # multiple: report expected transcript's first but flag\n                amp_len = amps_exp[0][2]\n        else:\n            # only off-target amplicons\n            t = matched[0]\n            amp_len = audit[\"matches\"][t][0][2]\n    ...\n```\n\nStatus decision tree:\n\n```python\n    if exp_rec is None:\n        status = \"expected_transcript_missing\"\n        status_reasons.append(f\"expected transcript {expected} not present in supplied FASTA\")\n    elif not matched:\n        status = \"no_binding\"\n        fwd_n = sum(1 for t in audit[\"fwd_hits\"] if audit[\"fwd_hits\"][t])\n        rev_n = ...\n        status_reasons.append(\"neither primer pair forms an amplicon on any supplied transcript\")\n    elif not amps_exp:\n        status = \"off_target\"\n        status_reasons.append(f\"amplicon only on non-expected transcript(s): {';'.join(matched)}\")\n    elif len(amps_exp) > 1 or len(audit[\"fwd_hits\"][expected]) > 1 or len(audit[\"rev_hits\"][expected]) > 1:\n        status = \"ambiguous\"\n    elif amps_exp[0][2] != expected_bp:\n        status = \"length_mismatch\"\n    else:\n        # length ok; check CDS\n        v = cds verdict\n        status = \"pass\" if cds ok else \"cds_incompatible\"\n```\n\nHmm for \"pass\" we'd also need cds_compatible to be yes; if metadata is inconsistent but length matches — status would be... length_mismatch takes precedence in our data anyway. Let me keep the branch: if amplicon length matches expected but cds is inconsistent → status \"metadata_conflict\"? Not needed here; let me implement it: if length ok but cds issue → status \"pass_with_metadata_flags\"? Over-engineering. Let me implement: length ok and cds_issues → status \"cds_metadata_conflict\"; length ok and no cds issue and within cds → \"pass\"; length ok and not within cds → \"cds_incompatible\".\n\nReason text construction: I'll build a detailed reason joining:\n- binding summary: e.g. \"Forward binds TX_CANONICAL at 1..20 (unique); reverse-complement of reverse primer binds TX_CANONICAL at 83..102 (unique).\"\n- amplicon: \"Amplicon TX_CANONICAL:1-102, length 102 bp; expected 108 bp (difference -6 bp).\"\n- specificity: \"No amplicon on TX_ALT.\"\n- metadata: \"TX_CANONICAL header CDS=101-700 is internally inconsistent: CDS end 700 exceeds sequence length 102; metadata reported as-is, not repaired.\"\n\nFor p03: \"Forward primer (dA20) matches no supplied transcript; reverse-complement of reverse primer (dA20) matches no supplied transcri